mainmenu "AD9081 JESD204B bring-up sample"

config AD9081_FAULT_INJECTION
	bool "Run JESD204 fault-injection tests after bring-up"
	help
	  Exercise the failure and recovery paths that a successful bring-up
	  never reaches: the FSM's failure accounting, the RX lane-desync
	  watchdog, link teardown followed by a second bring-up, and a GT
	  transceiver that cannot lock its PLL.

	  Enabling this compiles test-only hooks into axi_jesd204.c and
	  axi_adxcvr.c that let the tests force a lane-status value and
	  mis-program the RX GT clock mux. With it disabled (the default) none
	  of that code is built.

	  The tests deliberately break a working link and then rebuild it, so
	  do not leave this on in a normal build.

config AD9081_MXFE_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "AD9081/AD9082 MxFE init priority"
	default 60
	help
	  Initialisation priority of the AD9081 MxFE driver, within POST_KERNEL.

	  Must be above CONFIG_SPI_INIT_PRIORITY (default 50): the chip is
	  SPI-attached, and Zephyr SPI controllers initialise at POST_KERNEL.
	  There is a BUILD_ASSERT in ad9081.c for it.

	  Nothing more is required, because init() only marks the device ready --
	  the chip is programmed by explicit ad9081_probe() /
	  ad9081_setup_datapath() calls from the bring-up sequence, which run
	  from main() and therefore after every driver here.

config AD9081_ADXCVR_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "AXI-ADXCVR (GT transceiver) init priority"
	default 65
	help
	  Initialisation priority of the AXI-ADXCVR driver, within POST_KERNEL.

	  Must be above CONFIG_CLOCK_CONTROL_HMC7044_INIT_PRIORITY: the adxcvr
	  nodes take `clocks = <&hmc7044 12>`, and Zephyr's init-priority
	  validation rejects a device that initialises before a devicetree
	  dependency. There is a BUILD_ASSERT in axi_adxcvr.c for it.

config AD9081_JESD204_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "AXI-JESD204 link-core init priority"
	default 65
	help
	  Initialisation priority of the AXI-JESD204 RX/TX link-core driver,
	  within POST_KERNEL.

	  Unconstrained in practice, same as the transport cores: the nodes have
	  no devicetree dependencies (this port does not model the PL lane and
	  device clocks, so there is no clocks phandle), and init() only marks
	  the devices ready. The register work is an explicit
	  axi_jesd204_configure() call from the bring-up sequence.

config AD9081_TPL_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "AXI-AD9081 transport-core (TPL) init priority"
	default 65
	help
	  Initialisation priority of the AXI-AD9081 RX/TX transport-core driver,
	  within POST_KERNEL.

	  Unconstrained in practice: the nodes have no devicetree dependencies
	  (the transport cores run off the sample clock inside the PL, so there
	  is no clocks phandle), and init() only marks the devices ready. The
	  register work is an explicit axi_tpl_configure() call from the
	  bring-up sequence.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
